            <!-- Academy Guide Tab Content -->
            <div id="academy" class="tab-content">
                <div class="academy-guide">
                    <div class="theory-section">
                        <h3>Historical Context & Development</h3>
                        
                        <h4>Development Timeline</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>1992:</strong> Philippe Spalart and Steven Allmaras develop one-equation turbulence model at NASA Langley Research Center.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>1994:</strong> Original S-A model published, designed specifically for aerospace applications and external aerodynamics.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>1990s-2000s:</strong> Widespread adoption in aerospace industry, becomes standard for aircraft and missile design.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>2000s-Present:</strong> Various modifications (S-A-neg, S-A-RC, S-A-BC) developed for specific applications, though k-ω SST gains popularity.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4>Design Philosophy</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Single equation simplicity:</strong> Avoid complexity of two-equation models while maintaining reasonable accuracy.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Aerospace focus:</strong> Optimized for external aerodynamics, boundary layers, and mild separation.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Computational efficiency:</strong> Lower memory requirements and simpler implementation than k-ε or k-ω models.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Wall integration:</strong> Designed to integrate to the wall without wall functions, suitable for y⁺ ≈ 1.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="theory-section">
                        <h3>Mathematical Foundation & Transport Equation</h3>
                        
                        <h4>Modified Turbulent Viscosity Transport</h4>
                        <div class="equation-container">
                            <div class="equation-title">Spalart-Allmaras Transport Equation</div>
                            <div class="math-block">
                                $$\frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial t} + u_j \frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial x_j} = P + D - Y$$
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="equation-container">
                            <div class="equation-title">Complete Form with All Terms</div>
                            <div class="math-block">
                                $$\frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial t} + u_j \frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial x_j} = c_{b1} \tilde{S} \tilde{\nu} - \left[c_{w1} f_w - \frac{c_{b1}}{\kappa^2} f_{t2}\right] \left(\frac{\tilde{\nu}}{d}\right)^2$$
                            </div>
                            <div class="math-block">
                                $$+ \frac{1}{\sigma}\frac{\partial}{\partial x_j}\left[(\nu + \tilde{\nu}) \frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial x_j}\right] + \frac{c_{b2}}{\sigma}\left(\frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial x_j}\right)^2$$
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="physical-meaning">
                            <h4>Physical Interpretation</h4>
                            <p><strong>Modified turbulent viscosity ν̃:</strong> Related to but not identical to turbulent viscosity. Near walls, ν̃ ≈ νₜ. In the freestream, ν̃ can exceed νₜ significantly.</p>
                            <p><strong>Single equation approach:</strong> Avoids length scale equation (like ε or ω) by using distance to nearest wall as the length scale.</p>
                        </div>

                        <h4>Individual Term Definitions</h4>
                        <div class="definition-grid">
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">Production Term: $c_{b1} \tilde{S} \tilde{\nu}$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">
                                    $\tilde{S} = S + \frac{\tilde{\nu}}{\kappa^2 d^2} f_{v2}$ is modified strain rate.
                                    $S = \sqrt{2\Omega_{ij}\Omega_{ij}}$ is magnitude of vorticity tensor.
                                    Generates turbulent viscosity in regions of high strain.
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">Destruction Term: $c_{w1} f_w \left(\frac{\tilde{\nu}}{d}\right)^2$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">
                                    $f_w = g \left[\frac{1 + c_{w3}^6}{g^6 + c_{w3}^6}\right]^{1/6}$ where $g = r + c_{w2}(r^6 - r)$
                                    $r = \min\left(\frac{\tilde{\nu}}{\tilde{S}\kappa^2 d^2}, 10\right)$ is strain rate parameter.
                                    Destroys turbulent viscosity, preventing excessive buildup.
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">Diffusion: $\frac{1}{\sigma}\frac{\partial}{\partial x_j}\left[(\nu + \tilde{\nu}) \frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial x_j}\right]$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">
                                    Molecular and turbulent diffusion of modified turbulent viscosity.
                                    σ = 2/3 is turbulent Prandtl number for ν̃ equation.
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">Cross-Diffusion: $\frac{c_{b2}}{\sigma}\left(\frac{\partial \tilde{\nu}}{\partial x_j}\right)^2$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">
                                    Additional diffusion term proportional to gradient magnitude.
                                    $c_{b2} = 0.622$ enhances numerical stability and accuracy.
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="theory-section">
                        <h3>Turbulent Viscosity Calculation & Damping Functions</h3>
                        
                        <h4>Turbulent Viscosity Relation</h4>
                        <div class="equation-container">
                            <div class="equation-title">Viscosity Calculation</div>
                            <div class="math-block">
                                $$\nu_t = \tilde{\nu} f_{v1}, \quad f_{v1} = \frac{\chi^3}{\chi^3 + c_{v1}^3}, \quad \chi = \frac{\tilde{\nu}}{\nu}$$
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="physical-meaning">
                            <h4>Damping Function Behavior</h4>
                            <p><strong>Near wall (χ → 0):</strong> $f_{v1} → 0$, ensuring νₜ → 0 at the wall.</p>
                            <p><strong>Away from wall (χ >> 1):</strong> $f_{v1} → 1$, so νₜ ≈ ν̃ in turbulent regions.</p>
                            <p><strong>Transition region:</strong> Smooth transition ensures proper near-wall behavior without wall functions.</p>
                        </div>

                        <h4>Additional Damping Functions</h4>
                        <div class="definition-grid">
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$f_{v2} = 1 - \frac{\chi}{1 + \chi f_{v1}}$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">Modifies strain rate in production term to improve performance in adverse pressure gradients.</div>
                            </div>
                            
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$f_{t2} = c_{t3} \exp(-c_{t4} \chi^2)$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">Trip function term (usually zero in practical applications) for transition modeling.</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <h4>Model Constants</h4>
                        <div class="definition-grid">
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$c_{b1} = 0.1355$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">Production coefficient</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$c_{b2} = 0.622$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">Cross-diffusion coefficient</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$c_{w1} = \frac{c_{b1}}{\kappa^2} + \frac{1 + c_{b2}}{\sigma}$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">Destruction coefficient (≈ 3.239)</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$c_{w2} = 0.3, c_{w3} = 2.0$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">Destruction function parameters</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$c_{v1} = 7.1$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">Viscosity damping parameter</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="definition-item">
                                <div class="definition-term">$\kappa = 0.41, \sigma = 2/3$</div>
                                <div class="definition-desc">von Kármán constant and Prandtl number</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="theory-section">
                        <h3>Advanced Theoretical Framework & Assumptions</h3>
                        
                        <h4>Fundamental Assumptions</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Single length scale:</strong> Wall distance d provides length scale, eliminating need for separate length scale equation.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Boussinesq hypothesis:</strong> Maintains linear stress-strain relation with single turbulent viscosity.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Attached flow focus:</strong> Optimized for boundary layers and mildly separated flows, not free shear flows.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>High Reynolds number:</strong> Assumes turbulent flow, though near-wall treatment handles viscous effects.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4>Limitations and Modeling Compromises</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Geometric dependence:</strong> Requires wall distance calculation, complicating implementation in complex geometries.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Free shear flow weakness:</strong> Not calibrated for jets, mixing layers, or free turbulence.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Rotation effects:</strong> No built-in rotation or curvature corrections compared to more advanced models.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Scalar transport:</strong> Limited thermal modeling capabilities compared to k-ε family models.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="theory-section">
                        <h3>Wall Treatment & Boundary Conditions</h3>
                        
                        <h4>Wall Boundary Conditions</h4>
                        <div class="equation-container">
                            <div class="equation-title">Wall Conditions</div>
                            <div class="math-block">
                                $$\tilde{\nu}_{wall} = 0, \quad \nu_t = \tilde{\nu} f_{v1} → 0 \text{ as } \chi → 0$$
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="physical-meaning">
                            <h4>No-Slip Implementation</h4>
                            <p>The S-A model naturally satisfies no-slip conditions through the damping function $f_{v1}$. As ν̃ → 0 at the wall, χ → 0, and consequently νₜ → 0, ensuring proper wall treatment without explicit wall functions.</p>
                        </div>

                        <h4>Mesh Requirements</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>y⁺ ≈ 1:</strong> First cell should be in viscous sublayer for optimal accuracy.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Wall function alternative:</strong> Can use wall functions for y⁺ > 30, though less accurate than integration to wall.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Smooth growth:</strong> Boundary layer mesh should have smooth growth from wall to avoid numerical issues.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4>Far-Field Boundary Conditions</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Freestream specification:</strong> ν̃ = 0.1ν to 1.0ν in freestream (low values for external flows).</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Inlet conditions:</strong> Specify ν̃ based on expected turbulence level or empirical correlations.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Outlet conditions:</strong> Zero-gradient or convective boundary conditions for ν̃.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="theory-section">
                        <h3>Model Variants & Improvements</h3>
                        
                        <h4>Spalart-Allmaras Negative (S-A-neg)</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Negative viscosity handling:</strong> Allows ν̃ to become negative in separated regions, improving robustness.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Enhanced separation:</strong> Better prediction of flow separation and reattachment.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Modified destruction:</strong> Different destruction term formulation for negative ν̃ values.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4>Rotation and Curvature Correction (S-A-RC)</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Rotation effects:</strong> Includes corrections for system rotation and streamline curvature.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Production modification:</strong> Rotation function multiplies production term based on local rotation rate.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Improved accuracy:</strong> Better performance in rotating machinery and highly curved flows.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4>Boundary Condition Variant (S-A-BC)</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Modified wall condition:</strong> Alternative wall boundary condition implementation.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Enhanced robustness:</strong> Improved numerical stability in some flow configurations.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="theory-section">
                        <h3>Numerical Implementation & Solution Strategies</h3>
                        
                        <h4>Discretization Considerations</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Convection schemes:</strong> Second-order upwind recommended for ν̃ equation to balance accuracy and stability.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Source term treatment:</strong> Implicit treatment of destruction term prevents negative ν̃ in standard model.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Wall distance:</strong> Accurate and efficient wall distance calculation crucial for model performance.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4>Convergence Characteristics</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Single equation advantage:</strong> Generally converges faster than two-equation models.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Under-relaxation:</strong> Typical ν̃ under-relaxation factors: 0.7-1.0 depending on problem complexity.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Initialization:</strong> Initialize ν̃ field based on freestream conditions and expected boundary layer thickness.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                        <h4>Advanced Implementation Features</h4>
                        <ul class="guide-list">
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Adaptive wall distance:</strong> Efficient algorithms for wall distance computation in complex geometries.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Limiter functions:</strong> Prevent excessive production in stagnation regions and highly strained flows.</span>
                            </li>
                            <li class="guide-item">
                                <span class="guide-bullet">•</span>
                                <span class="guide-text"><strong>Compressibility corrections:</strong> Extensions for high-speed compressible flows in aerospace applications.</span>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Footer -->
    <footer class="footer">
        <div class="container">
